{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the read-only merge-plan recut at 88ac617cbfa9818418aaafab8b6d8b99832f655d (request/merge-plan-current, one commit on main c3169d83; exactly the thirteen cited paths, +1939/-452; Rests-On governing request 2b2c11ef; original request/merge-plan retained unmoved). Review against request 15131; not merged.\n\nWhat holds, all verified on a detached checkout. gs merge-plan (cmd/gs/main.go:729) and the MCP merge_plan tool (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:927) both call mergeplan.Build, and mergeCommand now derives its succession plan from the same Build, so the two surfaces cannot drift by construction; TestMergePlanToolMatchesSharedRefusal fails when the MCP delegation is removed and a DeepEqual pins the CLI. Read-only is proven empirically against the live workroom with a real ratified approval and candidate: all 286 refs, main, HEAD, every file under .git/gitseq and the target worktree are byte-identical before and after (exit 0, allowed true, mode fresh, 8.5 s), and the fixture hashes refs, objects, index, config and porcelain status; TestReadOnlySnapshotDoesNotRepairLaggingFrontierOrPublishCheckpoint and TestMergePlanRefusesDirtyCheckoutWithoutMutation go red under mutation. Refusals are reported as an exit-0 structured result. Flag parity holds in substance; the one difference (MCP checkout defaults to repo) is documented on both pages. Inputs are bounded: the candidate must be full lowercase hex and is resolved with rev-parse --verify --end-of-options; the approval reaches git only through --fixed-strings --grep; artifact paths never reach git or the filesystem; the disposable clone inherits no config or hooks. Gates: gofmt, go build, go vet, git diff --check clean; go test ./... -count=1 35 packages ok including cmd/gs (377 s), cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/app, internal/mergeplan and internal/docset (which runs the sh example in merge-plan.md).\n\nTwo changes before approval.\n\n1. The shared protection index dropped awaiting-merge. internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go:735 reads case \"open\", \"promised\", \"reported\", \"stale\"; the base and the now-dead copy at cmd/gs/succession.go:242 read \"open\", \"promised\", \"reported\", \"awaiting-merge\", \"stale\". awaiting-merge is a live status (twenty rows today; internal/statusview treats it as live). Run against the live workroom with only that word restored, the plan's classification of the nine SKILL.md covering artifacts changes: 12015ede, 4a6dc2fc, 6bfa9415 and c9173832 move from abandoned to protected sibling under live unsettled commitments, and e252a048 names a different protecting commitment. This is not confined to the diagnostic: cmd/gs/main.go:670-676 builds leftLive from prospective.CoveringArtifacts with no second computation, so the next gs merge would seal Gitseq-Left-Live and merge_left_live with a false, immutable statement that four live artifacts are abandoned, the very accounting AGENTS.md step 5 requires and on which an author is asked to retire work. Restore the status at mergeplan.go:735 and add a test pinning the list; today the mutation is green in both directions.\n\n2. Dead duplicated logic remains in cmd/gs and is the mechanism behind item 1. With zero production references at the head: coveredArtifacts, artifactCoversPath, protectionIndex, unsettledCommitment, provenanceClosure, withinReviewedPaths and artifactPath in cmd/gs/succession.go, and canonicalCommit, canonicalPath and verdictSequence in cmd/gs/main.go, about 130 lines; the seven Gitseq trailer constants, the receipt key and ref derivations and the receipt parser exist twice with no shared definition and no equality test; successionPredecessors and planSuccession are now test-only wrappers, so TestMergeDoesNotProtectThroughIneffectiveProvenance red-proves dead code. Delete the copies and share the constants.\n\nRecorded, not blocking: the merge-to-plan changed-path agreement check at cmd/gs/main.go:650, the only thing between a divergent clone-side resolution and a wrong sealed succession, has no red proof; the plan-side implementer, admission-ceiling and reviewed-scope checks (mergeplan.go:1093, 1151, 1144) have no red proof either, though merge re-enforces each, so the exposure is a wrong allowed true; ReadOnlySnapshot skips rememberVerifiedFrontier, so the plan does not apply the rollback and non-descendant-frontier refusal merge applies and can say allowed on a rolled-back log, worth a sentence in merge-plan.md; docs/reference/gs/merge.md does not list the three new merge refusals (target moved after planning, frontier moved after planning, tentative merge changed paths differ from the plan) and does not link merge-plan.md; neither new page rests on a cmd/gs artifact although gs merge-plan is cmd/gs behaviour and fresh current-main artifacts exist at cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp; resume-mode staleness is never rendered and readReceipt lacks the empty-trailer handling readMergeReceipt has, so a malformed receipt reads as legacy-allowed to the plan and is refused by merge; Result.Reasons is not in check order; the plan materialises a full working tree in TMPDIR, undocumented.\n\nArchitecture: new layer row internal/mergeplan plus amended cmd/gs row and read-only-snapshot paragraph are published in this head at docs/reference/architecture.md, and the described contract matches the code; the table lists internal/mergeplan above internal/service though it imports it, a placement nit with no new coupling. Contract changed and recorded in the same head, as step 4 requires.\n\nSecurity: read-only proven above; untrusted candidate, approval and artifact-path inputs are bounded as described; the result discloses only facts the same actor can already read and the signed prospective requests are discarded; authority is unchanged because the plan appends and reserves nothing. Two residuals: the plan is over-permissive on a rolled-back log (advisory above), and CanonicalCommit runs git -C on the caller-supplied checkout before the common-dir ownership check, pre-existing ordering unchanged from base. The blocking defect is item 1, a false durable audit record, not an authority breach.\n\nSimplification: item 2, plus the four missing red proofs above; nothing else duplicated beyond the copies named.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:79f15ec96e699f4b449f9ac250b50d5c4e8c8aa9","head":"88ac617cbfa9818418aaafab8b6d8b99832f655d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4af96777d339ef465dc589454091bd36cd643ddc\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:473142842618af91a386aaab73e585e341a7d69a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}